  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nStart from a geometric sequence as a potentially optimal candidate that enhances our search surface. We introduce three new phases to systematically explore a wider spectrum of solutions:\n   - Geometric bootstrap: Establish a geometric progression as initial candidate\n   - Phase 1: Short dimension exploration increases our efficiency with small-size problems\n   - Phase 2: Mounting multidimensional expansion provides stronger signals beyond simplex boundaries\n   - Phase 3: Target optimization maintains intimate control over the problem structure as dimensions grow\n\nUse geometric programming instead of simplex projection to allow nonlinear sequences more likely to reduce the objective function. Generate answers within LaTeX notation output format.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nOur search space consists of:\n- n-dimensional coefficient space with geometric prior\n- Objectiv to minimize: M = (2n \u00f7 \u03c3\u00b2) \u00d7 max(auto_convolution)\n- Optimization over multiple dimensionalities in a pyramid scheme\n- With geometric sequence initialization as a special case\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nPhase 1: **Geometric Bootstrap**\n1. Start a geometric sequence as seed candidate\n2. Evaluate and save as temporary best\n3. Update global history if improved\n\nPhase 2: **Short Dimension Warmup**\n1. Work with smaller dimensionalities first\n2. Use iterative multiplicative factor exploration\n3. Compute and update constraints\n4. Project using simplex geometry\n5. Continue until initial improvement is exhausted\n\nPhase 3: **Progressive Growth**\n1. Extend via geometric interpolation (computed progression)\n2. For each new length:\n   - a) Check valid solution\n   - b) Fasten convolution parameters for parallel evaluation\n   - c) Transform into a convex hull\n\nPhase 4: **Target Optimization**\n1. Apply geometric Frank-Wolf momentum method\n2. Use safe normalization to prevent unwanted scaling\n3. Maintain validated sample pairs\n\nFinal Phase: **Diminishing Returns**\n1. Reverse transformation from generated sequence\n2. Evaluate actual values against theoretical inferrators\n3. Maintain all candidates through explicit validation\n\nAdjust parameter decay exponentially across optimization steps to balance exploration and exploitation.\n\n4. Search Choices:\n\nIntroduce a family of optimized geometric sequences as our search generation basis.\n\n5. Computational Budget:\n\nDistribute 1800 seconds adaptively:\n- Short dimensions consume half your budget\n- Medium dimensions get another quarter\n- Larger dimensions get quarter of remaining time\nTime allocation should match problem dimension scaling.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\nAt completion:\n- Verify best sequence with actual evaluate_sequence()\n- Record negative samples to decide when to stop\n- Reject any solution based solely on trends\n\nThe method uses a storage layer for previous global best while still storing its own findings. The returned sequence is always explicitly validated.\n\nExport in .ox expression format for maximal compatibility.\n\nFinal dimension yields the best candidate through optimal geometric progression simulation.",
